rhodep-gnss
===========

What it does
------------

rhodep-gnss-daemon gets a position out of a phone whose satellite GNSS cannot
be used, and republishes it as ordinary NMEA:

    /run/gnss-share.sock     UNIX stream, mode 0660, group geoclue (or gps)
    tcp://127.0.0.1:2948     the same stream, for gpsd

Both accept any number of simultaneous readers. Writes are non-blocking and a
reader more than 64 KiB behind is dropped, so nothing a client does can stall
the daemon.

rhodep-cell-db builds the optional offline cell-tower database that the cell
source consults first.


Why there is no satellite fix on this phone
-------------------------------------------

Because trying to get one power-cycles the SoC.

Starting a QMI LOC session in operation mode standalone, default, msa or msb
resets this phone within 100 ms, reproducibly, with no panic and no oops --
the modem's GNSS measurement engine coming up is enough. Mode 'cellid' runs the
session state machine without ever starting that engine, and survives. So there
is no configuration of gpsd, geoclue or ModemManager that will produce a
satellite fix here; the only positions available are network-derived ones, and
that is what this package provides. See
docs/interconnect-sm6375-wip/GNSS-SM6375.md and scripts/rhodep-gnss-test.py in
the port repository for the bisection.

This is also why geoclue's modem-gps source is turned off in the shipped
configuration: it asks ModemManager to enable location, ModemManager enables
unassisted GNSS, and that is exactly the reset.


Where the position comes from
-----------------------------

--source picks the location source:

  wifi        Scan the WiFi neighbourhood and ask an Ichnaea-compatible
              geolocation service where those BSSIDs are. No SIM, no modem, no
              GNSS engine: this path cannot trip the reset bug even in
              principle, because it never opens the QMI device at all.
              MEASURED here: 23 m.

  cell        Read which GERAN/UMTS/LTE cells the modem can hear, and resolve
              those identities to a position -- first against a local
              OpenCelliD extract, then against the same geolocation services.
              Works with an unprovisioned SIM and a modem that never registers.
              Allocates a QMI NAS client; never touches QMI LOC.
              MEASURED here: 250 m.

  qmi-cellid  One QMI LOC session on qrtr://0, pinned to operation mode
              'cellid'. This is the modem resolving its own serving cell
              internally, which it will only do once it is registered -- in
              practice, once it has a provisioned SIM. Unavailable here.

  auto        The default, and what the shipped unit runs. Reads modem
              registration state from ModemManager over D-Bus and uses
              qmi-cellid only while the modem is actually registered;
              otherwise runs wifi and cell together and publishes whichever
              has a current fix, preferring wifi. Re-checks every --recheck
              seconds, so a SIM that gets provisioned later is picked up
              without a restart.

The priority in 'auto' is qmi-cellid, then wifi, then cell. That is an ordering
by how directly each answer is tied to where the phone is, not by how often it
works:

    qmi-cellid   the modem's own resolved position
    wifi         several access points a few tens of metres away, trilaterated
    cell         one tower up to a few kilometres away; the answer is the
                 tower's surveyed position, not yours

A source only wins if its fix is current (--stale, --wifi-stale, --cell-stale)
and honest (see "Not over-claiming accuracy"). So a stale WiFi fix yields to a
current cell fix automatically, and no fix at all is published if the only
candidate is too coarse to describe truthfully.


Which QMI services get opened, and why that is the safety story
---------------------------------------------------------------

    --source=wifi         no QMI at all
    --source=cell         QMI NAS  (read-only)
    --source=auto         QMI NAS  (read-only), plus QMI LOC only once the
                          modem is registered
    --source=qmi-cellid   QMI LOC

The thing that resets this SoC is QMI_LOC_START in a mode that brings up the
measurement engine. QMI NAS is the network-access service: get-cell-location-
info and get-serving-system report what the radio has already measured while
looking for a network. They start nothing. They have been run on this phone
several hundred times during development with no uptime reset.

The registration check that gates the LOC path is a plain D-Bus property read
on org.freedesktop.ModemManager1.Modem.Modem3gpp. It does not open the QMI
device, allocate a LOC client, or go anywhere near the location engine. With an
unregistered modem, 'auto' means no LOC session is ever started, so the reset
bug is out of reach rather than merely guarded against.

--no-cell removes even the NAS client, if you want a daemon that provably
speaks no QMI outside --source=qmi-cellid.


The three operation-mode gates, and why they are state and not a chain
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

QMI_LOC_START is sent from exactly one function, start_session(). It is reached
only when all three of these have said 'cellid':

    gate 1   the set_operation_mode request is acked
    gate 2   the set-operation-mode INDICATION reports SUCCESS
    gate 3   get-operation-mode reads the mode back as 'cellid'

Gate 1 alone is not enough: the LOC service acks the request and reports the
real outcome in the indication, so a mode that was accepted but never applied
leaves the engine in standalone -- which reboots the phone.

These used to be a chain: ack callback -> indication -> readback -> start. That
was wrong, and the fault injection is what showed it. MEASURED on this modem,
2026-09-05, with --test-gate-failure=indication:

    setting LOC operation mode to cellid
    --test-gate-failure=indication: simulating a failed indication gate
    REFUSING TO START A GNSS SESSION: modem refused operation mode cellid
    set_operation_mode acked, waiting for the indication     <- ack arrived LAST

The indication is delivered before the response, every time. So the chain
advanced to the readback -- and could have reached QMI_LOC_START -- before gate
1 had produced a verdict at all, and a late ack failure would have been
reported after the session was already running. Each gate now records its
verdict, _after_set_mode() advances only when gates 1 and 2 have both landed in
whatever order they arrive, and start_session() re-asserts all three plus the
operation mode before it sends anything. A missing gate is a refusal, not a
session.

refuse() also drops the state machine back to idle before doing anything else,
so a reply still in flight for a gate that has already failed cannot find a
state it recognises and restart the sequence.

Proving it, without a modem that misbehaves:

    rhodep-gnss-daemon --source qmi-cellid --test-gate-failure=ack
    rhodep-gnss-daemon --source qmi-cellid --test-gate-failure=indication
    rhodep-gnss-daemon --source qmi-cellid --test-gate-failure=readback

Each must print REFUSING TO START A GNSS SESSION and must never print "starting
LOC session". --self-test covers the same preconditions without hardware.

--operation-mode and --no-mode-readback both require
--i-know-this-reboots-the-phone. The second one is not obviously a safety flag
-- it only removes gate 3 -- but removing a gate on a phone whose failure mode
is a power cycle is exactly what that flag is for.


ModemManager is the other way in
--------------------------------

The daemon can only guard its own path. ModemManager reaches the same
measurement engine and has no gates at all. MEASURED here:

    $ mmcli -m any --location-status
      Location | capabilities: 3gpp-lac-ci, gps-raw, gps-nmea, agps-msa, agps-msb
               |      enabled: 3gpp-lac-ci

gps-raw, gps-nmea, agps-msa and agps-msb all reset this SoC, and all four are
one D-Bus call to Location.Setup() away -- from a settings panel, a phone
shell's location toggle, geoclue's modem-gps source, or `mmcli
--location-enable-gps-nmea`. So this package ships

    /usr/share/dbus-1/system.d/zz-rhodep-no-modem-gnss.conf

a mandatory D-Bus deny of Location.Setup, Location.InjectAssistanceData and
Location.SetSuplServer. context="mandatory" because ModemManager's own policy
allows root everything and D-Bus applies default < user < mandatory; the
accident being prevented is very much a `sudo mmcli` one. GetLocation and
SetGpsRefreshRate are left alone: neither can start the engine. Verify with

    $ mmcli -m any --location-enable-3gpp
    error: ... Rejected send message ...

(that call is safe to run either way: it asks for the LAC/CI source, which is
already enabled and touches no GNSS.) To undo on a fixed kernel, delete the
file and `systemctl reload dbus`.

What this does NOT stop is ModemManager probing the LOC service by itself while
initialising a modem, to fill in the capability list above:

    <wrn> [modem0] couldn't load SUPL server: Failed to receive indication ...
    <wrn> [modem0] couldn't load supported assistance data types: Failed to
                   receive indication with the predicted orbits data source

Those are LOC reads (QMI_LOC_GET_SERVER and the predicted-orbits/XTRA data
source query), they happen on every modem enumeration, there is no ModemManager
configuration in 1.24 that disables them, and they have been observed on this
phone several times without a reset. What can be controlled is how often a
modem gets enumerated -- see below.


rhodep-qmi-proxy.service
------------------------

libqmi clients that open a device with the PROXY flag share one
/usr/libexec/qmi-proxy, forked on demand by whichever client finds the socket
missing first. MEASURED, 2026-09-05: that was rhodep-gnss.service, so the proxy
lived in its cgroup, and the default KillMode=control-group meant every
`systemctl restart rhodep-gnss` killed ModemManager's transport with it:

    19:33:08  ModemManager: Cannot read from istream: connection broken
    19:33:08  ModemManager: [modem0] port 'qrtr0' no longer controllable, reprobing
    19:33:26  ModemManager: [modem1] couldn't load SUPL server: ...
    19:33:36  ModemManager: [modem1] couldn't load supported assistance data types: ...

i.e. restarting the location daemon re-enumerated the modem and made
ModemManager re-probe the LOC service. rhodep-qmi-proxy.service owns the proxy
instead, so neither daemon forks one and neither can take the other's transport
down. rhodep-gnss.service also carries KillMode=process as a fallback for the
case where that unit is masked.


The wifi source
---------------

Scanning. Two backends, --wifi-backend:

  nm    NetworkManager over D-Bus (default, tried first). NM is already
        scanning to keep its own AP list warm, so reading it is free. When
        that list is too old or too thin -- which it is right after a boot, and
        while associated NM only rescans every couple of minutes -- the daemon
        asks NM for a scan via RequestScan and waits for LastScan to move.
        Going through NM means NM schedules the scan around the active
        connection instead of two things fighting over the radio.

  iw    `iw dev IF scan dump` for exact dBm out of the kernel BSS cache,
        falling back to a full `iw dev IF scan`. Needs root. NM reports signal
        as a 0-100 quality which has to be converted back to dBm and comes out
        about 5 dB pessimistic; iw gives the real number. The services only use
        signal to weight APs against each other, so it rarely matters.

Access points whose SSID ends in _nomap are excluded, per the opt-out
convention. Nothing is ever submitted back to any service: submission would
need a trustworthy GPS fix to tie the BSSIDs to, and the only fix this phone
can produce is itself derived from a geolocation service.

Fewer than two visible access points produces "only 0 usable AP(s) and 0
cell(s); need 2 AP(s) or 1 cell(s)". Two is Ichnaea's minimum; with one AP you
are really just asking the service to geolocate your ISP.


The cell source
---------------

Reading the cells. The modem is asked, every --cell-poll seconds (default 15),
for QMI NAS get-cell-location-info. On this phone that is:

    $ qmicli -p -d qrtr://0 --nas-get-cell-location-info
    GERAN Info
        Cell ID: '60858'
        PLMN: '72234'
        Location Area Code: '420'
        GERAN Absolute RF Channel Number: '179'
        Base Station Identity Code: '19'
        Timing Advance: 'unavailable'
        RX Level: -97 dBm > level > -96 dBm ('14')
        Cell [0]:
            Cell ID: '40012'
            PLMN: '72234'
            Location Area Code: '421'
            GERAN Absolute RF Channel Number: '239'
            Base Station Identity Code: '4'
            RX Level: -103 dBm > level > -102 dBm ('8')

i.e. MCC 722, MNC 34, LAC 420, CID 60858 for the serving cell, and a decoded
neighbour on LAC 421. Note that this works with a SIM the network rejects and a
modem that reports "not-registered-searching": a modem measures and decodes the
broadcast identity of the cells around it as part of searching, whether or not
it ever registers.

ModemManager's own --location-get reports nothing here, because MM only
populates 3GPP LAC/CI from a *registered* serving cell. Reading NAS directly
bypasses that. (3gpp-lac-ci is enabled in MM regardless; it makes no
difference.)

Intermittency, and why the reader is separate from the query. The same command
run ten times in a row returns anything from a full identity to
"NoNetworkFound" to PLMN 00-00 to a neighbour whose ARFCN and signal are known
but whose Cell ID reads 'unavailable' -- a neighbour is measured several
seconds before its BCCH has been read. Observed hit rates on this phone have
ranged from 2/10 while the modem was actively cycling through bands to 10/10
once it settled on a cell in limited service. So:

  * CellReader polls on the short --cell-poll timer, keeps the last good
    answer, and records how old it is;
  * CellSource asks the providers every --cell-interval (default 60) using
    whatever the reader has, provided it is newer than --cell-max-age
    (default 300);
  * anything with cell id 0, LAC 0 or an all-ones field is discarded, not
    submitted. Asking a database about cell zero is asking for a wrong answer.

Backends, --cell-backend: 'qmi' talks to libqmi in process through
gir1.2-qmi-1.0; 'qmicli' shells out to the qmicli binary from libqmi-utils;
'auto' (default) tries qmi and falls back to qmicli. They send the same message.
One known difference: pygobject cannot marshal libqmi's fixed-size PLMN field
inside the neighbour-cell struct ("Array type is assumed to be a pointer, but
item size is 1 bytes"), so the in-process backend assumes neighbours are on the
serving PLMN -- true by construction for a GERAN BA list -- while the qmicli
backend reads each neighbour's PLMN exactly.

Providers, tried in order:

  1. the offline OpenCelliD extract, /var/lib/rhodep-gnss/cells.sqlite, if one
     has been built. First because it is free, instant, private and works with
     the WAN down.
  2. BeaconDB, https://api.beacondb.net/v1/geolocate
  3. Positon, https://api.positon.xyz/v1/geolocate?key=...

MEASURED on 2026-09-05 in Rosario/AR, for GSM 722-34 LAC 420 CID 60858:

    offline (OpenCelliD 2024-05 + MLS 2024-03, 652905 cells for MCC 722)
                                    miss - the identity is not in either dump
    BeaconDB                        HTTP 404, no coverage
    Positon                         -32.952961, -60.643772, accuracy 250 m

The Positon answer is 137 m from the WiFi fix taken at the same moment, which
is consistent with a 250 m claim. A second, independently decoded neighbour
cell (LAC 421 CID 40012) also resolves, to -32.955648, -60.645272 at 159 m.

About OpenCelliD's API. https://opencellid.org/cell/get needs an API access
token; without one it answers {"error":"API Key not known: ","code":2} with
HTTP 200, and an invalid token gets the same. There is no anonymous or keyless
mode. Tokens are free, but registration is a web form at
https://my.opencellid.org/register asking for a name and an e-mail address and
protected by reCAPTCHA, so one cannot be obtained non-interactively. This
package therefore does not ship a token and does not query the OpenCelliD API.
If you have one, put it in TOKEN= in /etc/default/rhodep-cell-db and
rhodep-cell-db will use the official, current, per-country export instead of
the archived mirror.


The offline cell database
-------------------------

    rhodep-cell-db build --mcc 722 --source ocid,mls    # needs root to write
    rhodep-cell-db info
    rhodep-cell-db lookup --mcc 722 --mnc 34 --lac 420 --cid 60858 --radio gsm
    rhodep-cell-db self-test

Both binaries live in /usr/local/sbin, which is NOT in sudo's secure_path on
this image (Defaults secure_path="/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin"), so anything
run under sudo needs the full path:

    sudo /usr/local/sbin/rhodep-cell-db build --mcc 722 --source ocid,mls

info, lookup and self-test are read-only and do not need root at all.

Sources, all keyless:

  ocid  Internet Archive mirror of the OpenCelliD full export, 2024-05-19,
        CC-BY-SA 4.0. 1.1 GB compressed.
  mls   Internet Archive copy of the final Mozilla Location Service cell
        export, 2024-03-27, public domain. 1.6 GB compressed. Frozen for good,
        MLS having shut down in 2024, but it was an independent survey.

Comma-separate them to merge; the first one named wins where they disagree.
The dump is streamed and filtered on the fly, so nothing is spooled to disk and
memory stays bounded regardless of dump size. MEASURED for MCC 722:

    ocid alone      312146 cells
    ocid,mls        652905 cells, 29.2 MiB of SQLite, 530 s over a 5 MB/s link
                    (206603 UMTS / 53383 GSM / 52160 LTE from ocid, plus
                     340759 more from mls)

A prebuilt database is NOT shipped in this package, deliberately. See the next
section for why it would be misleading here, and note that it would also be
29 MiB of Argentina in a package installed on phones that are not in Argentina.

rhodep-cell-db.timer refreshes it monthly and is shipped DISABLED, because a
refresh streams over a gigabyte and a package should not decide to do that on
someone's phone:

    systemctl enable --now rhodep-cell-db.timer

Configure it in /etc/default/rhodep-cell-db (MCC, SOURCE, DB, TOKEN). Note that
with the default archived sources a refresh is pure waste -- they are static
snapshots that will never change -- so building once by hand is usually the
right answer. The timer becomes worth enabling once TOKEN= is set.

Lookups are exact on (mcc, mnc, lac, cid), with the radio type used when it
matches and ignored when it does not, since a hit on the same identity under a
different radio is still an exact identity. There is deliberately no "nearest
cell in this location area" mode: an LAC centroid is the lacf answer the daemon
refuses from the network providers, and it would be no more honest for coming
out of a local file. When several observed cells resolve, the answer is their
inverse-variance-weighted centroid and the accuracy is the largest of
(distance from the answer to that cell) + (that cell's coverage radius) over
all of them -- a circle that contains every contributing cell's entire claimed
coverage, rather than an average that no measurement supports.


Why the offline database misses this phone's own cell
------------------------------------------------------

This is worth stating plainly, because it looks like a bug and is not.

The 2024 OpenCelliD export has 4572 cells within 3 km of this flat. The area is
very well surveyed. But its GSM cells for operator 722-34 here are under LAC
400, with cell ids 60213, 40213, 40012, 30213, 10012 and so on, while the modem
today reports LAC 420 CID 60858 and LAC 421 CID 40012. Telecom Argentina
appears to have renumbered its GERAN location areas since the dump was taken.
CID 60858 does not appear anywhere in MCC 722 in either dump; the only 60858 in
Argentina belongs to operator 722-07, in Neuquén, 800 km away -- which is
exactly why matching is on the full (mcc, mnc, lac, cid) tuple and never on
cell id alone.

A stale dump does not degrade gracefully into a worse fix. It simply does not
contain the key you are looking for. That is the whole argument for keeping a
provider chain instead of picking one provider.

OpenCelliD's own exports are limited to the last 18 months of observations,
which is why a 2024 mirror is what is publicly available without a token; the
OpenCelliD API has no such limit, which is a reason to get a token if you can.

For completeness, one other keyless cell API was measured and rejected:
api.mylnikov.org answers for this cell, but with a "range" of 18.7 km at a
position 72 km from the truth -- not even self-consistent. It is not in the
chain. --max-accuracy would refuse it anyway.


Fusing WiFi and cell
--------------------

--fuse sends the WiFi scan and the cell list in one Ichnaea request. Both
BeaconDB and Positon accept that. It is off by default for two reasons: the
reply does not say which array it matched, so the resulting fix cannot be
attributed to a source, and MEASURED here it changes nothing -- a fused query
returns the same 23 m answer as the WiFi-only query while WiFi works, and when
WiFi stops working the separate cell source produces its own fix anyway.


The considerIp trap
-------------------

Every geolocate request this daemon sends contains "considerIp": false, and
that is not a detail.

Leave it out and BeaconDB answers HTTP 200 with a location and
"fallback": "ipf". That is DB-IP geolocation of your egress IP address. On this
phone it is 25 km wrong, and if the phone is behind a VPN or a carrier NAT it
can be a different country. Nothing about the reply says "this is a guess"
except that one field, and an HTTP 200 with plausible coordinates looks exactly
like a success.

So: considerIp is false, and any reply that comes back flagged ipf (IP address)
or lacf (location area centroid) is refused anyway, as is anything coarser than
--max-accuracy (default 5000 m). --allow-fallback turns all of that off if you
have a reason to want it.


Trying it by hand
-----------------

    rhodep-gnss-daemon --locate-once     # one wifi scan + one query, printed
    sudo /usr/local/sbin/rhodep-gnss-daemon --cells-once
                                         # one cell read + the provider chain
    rhodep-gnss-daemon --self-test       # 112 checks, no hardware, no network

--cells-once needs root, for AF_QIPCRTR, and the full path, because
/usr/local/sbin is not in sudo's secure_path on this image.

--locate-once and --cells-once each do exactly one lookup, print what they
submitted, the coordinates, the accuracy, what a consumer bucketing our HDOP
will conclude, an OpenStreetMap link and the NMEA that would have been emitted,
then exit. Neither opens a socket. --cells-once opens QMI NAS read-only and
prints the per-provider outcome:

    provider chain:
      - opencellid-offline: none of the 1 cell(s) are in the dump
      - api.beacondb.net: HTTP 404 (no coverage for these 1 cell(s))
      - api.positon.xyz: hit


Why the operation mode matters
------------------------------

(All of this applies to --source=qmi-cellid, and to --source=auto once the
modem is registered. In wifi and cell modes none of it runs.)

The check is deliberately paranoid, because the failure mode is a reboot:

  1. QMI_LOC_SET_OPERATION_MODE must be acked, and
  2. the set-operation-mode *indication* must come back SUCCESS - the ack alone
     only says the request was accepted, and
  3. QMI_LOC_GET_OPERATION_MODE must read the mode back as cellid.

A set that is acked but not applied leaves the engine in standalone, which is
exactly the case that reboots the phone, so "assume it worked" is not an option
here.

If any of those fails the daemon logs "REFUSING TO START A GNSS SESSION" and
exits 3 without ever sending QMI_LOC_START. That path can be exercised without
waiting for a modem that misbehaves:

    rhodep-gnss-daemon --test-gate-failure=ack        --socket '' --tcp 2960
    rhodep-gnss-daemon --test-gate-failure=indication --socket '' --tcp 2961
    rhodep-gnss-daemon --test-gate-failure=readback   --socket '' --tcp 2962

Exit 3 rather than 1 so systemd can tell a decision from a fault:
rhodep-gnss.service carries RestartPreventExitStatus=3, so a refusal leaves the
unit visibly failed instead of restarting into the same refusal forever. That
replaced a StartLimitBurst=5, which counted every start including successful
ones and `systemctl restart`, and MEASURED here locked the daemon out for five
minutes after five ordinary restarts, taking /run/gnss-share.sock with it.

--i-know-this-reboots-the-phone unlocks --operation-mode for future testing,
and is also required by --no-mode-readback, which removes gate 3. Do not use
either on hardware you want to stay up.


The sentences
-------------

In cellid mode the modem's own NMEA output is a bare $PSTIS, once per second,
which gpsd and geoclue can do nothing with, and the network sources produce no
NMEA at all. So the daemon builds $GPGGA, $GPRMC and $GPGSA at 1 Hz from
whichever position it has, with correct checksums. The modem's own sentences
are passed through as well unless --no-passthrough is given.

GGA fix quality is reported as 1 ("GPS fix") when there is a position. NMEA
0183 has no code for a network/cell-id/WiFi fix and gpsd treats 0 as no fix at
all, so 1 is the only value that makes the fix visible; the satellite count
stays 0.

$GPGST is emitted as well whenever the source knows its real accuracy, which
the geolocation services and the offline database both do. GST is the only NMEA
sentence that carries an error estimate in metres rather than a dimensionless
DOP. gpsd 3.27.5 parses it and republishes it verbatim as its own report class,
but, measured here, does not fold it into TPV: TPV's eph stays HDOP times
gpsd's 19 m UERE. So a client that wants the real number reads the GST report;
a client that reads TPV.eph gets a conservative overestimate. Neither is told
the fix is better than it is, which is the property that matters.


Not over-claiming accuracy
--------------------------

A network fix has no satellite geometry, so whatever goes in the HDOP field is
a fabrication. The question is which fabrication. Consumers bucket HDOP into
quality bands; geoclue 2.8.1, measured on this phone by feeding it synthetic
GGA at fixed HDOP, maps them to:

    HDOP <=1 -> 0 m    <=2 -> 1 m     <=5 -> 3 m
         <=10 -> 50 m  <=20 -> 100 m   >20 -> 300 m

Scaling accuracy by the conventional 5 m per DOP is therefore actively
harmful: a 22 m WiFi fix becomes HDOP 4.4, lands in the "good" band, and
geoclue reports 3 m -- a sevenfold overstatement. So instead the daemon picks
the lowest band whose implied accuracy is still at least as large as the
accuracy it was actually given, and emits an HDOP from inside it. Consumers
that bucket HDOP round the error up, never down; consumers that read GST get
the exact metres anyway.

Cell-tower fixes make that table's ceiling matter. It saturates at 300 m, so
there is no HDOP -- no value at all -- that tells geoclue "1200 m". Any fix
coarser than 300 m would reach geoclue as a claim of 300 m, with nothing in the
stream to contradict it. $GPGST would still carry the truth, but geoclue does
not read GST: `strings /usr/libexec/geoclue` on this phone contains "$GPGGA"
and nothing else, and gclue-nmea-source.c only ever parses GGA.

So the daemon refuses to publish a fix whose accuracy exceeds
--max-nmea-accuracy, which defaults to exactly that 300 m ceiling. It logs why,
once:

    cell fix from api.positon.xyz is 1200m, coarser than the 300m that $GPGGA's
    HDOP field can honestly express; not publishing it (raise or disable
    --max-nmea-accuracy to override)

--max-nmea-accuracy 0 disables the check, for anyone who would rather have a
coarse position than none. The GST metres stay honest either way.

The resulting invariant, which --self-test checks directly, is: for every fix
this daemon publishes, the accuracy a consumer infers from the HDOP is at least
the accuracy the source actually reported.

MEASURED at this location:

                            WiFi fix            cell fix
    truth (Positon)             23 m              250 m
    $GPGST                      23 m              250 m   exact
    geoclue                     50 m              300 m
    gpsd TPV eph               142 m              475 m   HDOP x 19 m UERE
    cgps 2D Err                467 ft            1558 ft

Every number in every column is at least the truth. That is the intended
outcome; the alternative ordering, where a consumer believes 3 m, is not
recoverable downstream.


Timestamps
----------

Sentences carry the time they are sent, not the time the position was
obtained -- except for QMI positions less than two seconds old, which keep the
modem's own more precise UTC. The network sources only produce a position every
--wifi-interval / --cell-interval, and re-broadcasting one at 1 Hz under its
original timestamp freezes the clock in the stream; gpsd reads that as one
endlessly repeated epoch and stops propagating the fix to clients. Stamping
with the current time says "this is where you are now", which is the claim a
network fix is making anyway.

When there is no position - or the last one is older than the relevant staleness
limit - the sentences are still emitted, with RMC status V and GGA quality 0.
That keeps gpsd and geoclue attached to a live source instead of a silent
socket.


When there is no position
-------------------------

qmi-cellid needs a registered modem. If the modem is not registered (no SIM, or
a SIM the network rejects - check `mmcli -m 0` for "registration: searching"
and any "network rejection error") every position report comes back with
session status general-failure and no coordinates. --source=auto avoids this
case entirely by not using the QMI LOC path at all until the modem registers.

WiFi geolocation fails when the services have no data for the BSSIDs in range.
The daemon logs the reason once rather than every interval, since "this area
is not in the database" does not change from minute to minute:

    wifi geolocation failed: api.beacondb.net: HTTP 404 (no coverage for
    these 12 BSSID(s)); api.positon.xyz: HTTP 404 ...

Cell geolocation fails the same way, and additionally when the modem has not
decoded any identity yet:

    cell reader: modem reported no decoded cell identity (it is still searching)
    cell geolocation failed: opencellid-offline: none of the 1 cell(s) are in
    the dump; api.beacondb.net: HTTP 404 ...

If every configured provider says that, cell positioning genuinely cannot work
for that cell and there is no setting that will fix it. It is a legitimate
outcome, and it is why the daemon has three sources rather than one.

In all of these cases the daemon runs normally and publishes valid no-fix
sentences, keeping gpsd and geoclue attached to a live source.


Checking it
-----------

    systemctl status rhodep-gnss
    journalctl -u rhodep-gnss -f

    socat -u UNIX-CONNECT:/run/gnss-share.sock -      # the UNIX feed
    nc 127.0.0.1 2948                                 # the TCP feed
    gpspipe -r ; gpspipe -w                           # through gpsd
    cgps -s

netcat-traditional (the /usr/bin/nc on this image) has no -U, hence socat.

    /usr/local/sbin/rhodep-gnss-daemon --self-test    # NMEA generator, scan
                                                      # and cell-info parsers,
                                                      # PLMN decoding, provider
                                                      # request shapes, source
                                                      # priority, accuracy
                                                      # policy. No hardware,
                                                      # no network.
    /usr/local/sbin/rhodep-cell-db self-test          # builder and lookups

Note that geoclue behaves differently for uid 0; run geoclue client tools as an
ordinary user or they will hang waiting for a client that never gets created.

gpsd
----

gpsd cannot read UNIX sockets, only serial devices and tcp://|udp:// URLs, so
it gets the TCP feed. The package ships
/usr/lib/systemd/system/gpsd.service.d/10-rhodep-gnss.conf, which sets

    DEVICES=tcp://127.0.0.1:2948
    GPSD_OPTIONS=-n

and, because drop-ins are merged after the unit's own EnvironmentFile=, wins
over /etc/default/gpsd. The postinst writes the same values into
/etc/default/gpsd as well, but only while that file is still at the pristine
Debian default, and keeps a .rhodep-gnss.bak that postrm restores.

geoclue
-------

/etc/geoclue/conf.d/20-rhodep-wifi.conf:

  * turns on network-nmea and points it at /run/gnss-share.sock, which is what
    actually delivers a position here;
  * turns on the wifi source and pins its URL to BeaconDB. geoclue 2.8 already
    defaults to BeaconDB, so this is mostly making it explicit -- and note
    that geoclue's wifi source takes exactly one URL and cannot fail over,
    which is why the BeaconDB-then-Positon chain lives in the daemon instead.
    Where BeaconDB has no coverage this source simply produces nothing;
  * turns off modem-gps, on purpose. It asks ModemManager to enable location,
    ModemManager enables unassisted GNSS, and that is the thing that reboots
    this phone;
  * turns off the ip source, whose answer here is the ISP's city at 25 km. It
    would otherwise hand applications a plausible-looking wrong answer in
    exactly the situation this package exists to fix;
  * marks a few command-line clients as system components, so they get a
    location without an agent to authorize them. Without this a tool with no
    .desktop file blocks until it times out.

geoclue is dbus-activated, so it picks up config changes on its next start:

    systemctl stop geoclue        # next client gets the new configuration

Predicted orbits (gpsOneXTRA)
-----------------------------

Stock Android runs xtra-daemon (ENABLED in the stock izat.conf) to download
Qualcomm's predicted-orbit almanac and inject it into the modem. This port had
no equivalent, so the modem's almanac read the GPS epoch -- never loaded:

    valid from 1980-01-06T00:00:00Z, valid for 0 hours

rhodep-xtra is that missing daemon, run by rhodep-xtra.timer 3 minutes after
boot and every 6 hours thereafter. It downloads ~40 kB.

    rhodep-xtra status     read-only LOC state inventory; changes nothing
    rhodep-xtra fetch      download the almanac only
    rhodep-xtra inject     fetch if stale, then inject orbits + time + position
    rhodep-xtra self-test  offline; 33 checks, touches no hardware

XTRA is predicted ephemeris: about a week of computed satellite orbits, so the
receiver need not demodulate ephemeris off the satellites at 50 bit/s before it
can compute a fix. It shortens time-to-first-fix. It does not itself produce a
fix, and on this phone the satellite engine is still the thing that resets the
SoC -- see "There is no satellite fix to be had on this phone" above. This is
here because it is a real missing piece of the port, and because it is needed
before the reset can be tested against an engine that has everything stock
gives it.

It cannot start the measurement engine. The only QMI message that does is
QMI_LOC_START (0x0022), which carries a Session ID and a Fix Recurrence.
rhodep-xtra sends only assistance injection -- what A-GPS does before a fix --
and never sends QMI_LOC_REGISTER_EVENTS. This is enforced by an allow-list
asserted on every send, not merely intended; the self-test checks that start,
stop, register_events and delete_assistance_data are all unsendable.

Also the only way to see the SUPL server on this port: qmicli has no
--loc-get-server, though libqmi defines the message. It reads
supl.google.com:7275 here, which is Motorola's factory value still living in
the modem's EFS.

